# Overview
#
# Reports `may` where it mostly states a possibility (`the cache may be
# stale`). `may` reads two ways, as a permission (`the caller may pass
# nil`) or as a possibility, so the reader has to pick. The
# technical-writing skill keeps `may` for an option a person is free to
# take and writes `might` for a possibility. The fix writes `might` for
# a possibility and `can` for an ability or a permission. The rule sits
# at Recommended, because some hits state an ability or a permission,
# where `might` is the wrong fix.
#
# Expression
#
# The rule counts `may` after any word but `you` and before a verb or an
# adverb. An adverb is a word that describes a verb, an adjective, or a
# whole sentence, such as `safely` or `also`. A guard on the word after
# `may` skips `not`, `want`, `possibly`, and `potentially`.
#
# Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes the word before `may`
# and narrows the finding back to `may`. A sentence that opens with
# `May` is a question or a date, so it passes.
#
# False Positives
#
# `you may` passes, because there `may` gives a person an option (`You
# may skip the tag`). The verb or adverb after `may` keeps out the
# month, which a number, a comma, or a noun follows (`in May 2026`, `the
# May release`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The guard on the word after `may` skips the phrases that other rules
# report. ProseMayNot reports `may not` and `may or may not`. The
# Recommended ProseObligation reports `may want to`. ProseDoubleHedge
# reports `may possibly` and `may potentially`.
#
# ProseCapsEmphasis also reports an uppercase `MAY`, and that finding
# takes a second fix.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 976 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 14 of 20 sampled
# hits stated a possibility. Five stated a permission (`the
# administrator may specify a default StorageClass`), where `can` reads
# the same. The one misfire was `MAY` in a quoted sentence of an RFC,
# which the writer cannot reword.
expr skip [not, want, possibly, potentially]
expr main <(!you may <[VERB, AUX, ADV], !@skip>), may>
let message "`may` for a possibility. Write `might`, or `can` for an ability."
let description "Flags `may` outside `you may`, where it usually states a possibility that `might` states in one reading."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "You may skip the tag."
allows "The cache might be stale after a restart."
allows "The name may not be a dot."
allows "You may want to pin the version."
allows "Version 2 shipped in May."
